1. What is a Stack? (Core Concept)A Stack is a linear data structure that follows the LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle. The last element added to the stack is always the first one to be removed.Single Entry Point (Top): All additions (push) and removals (pop) happen exclusively at one end, called the Top.Restricted Access: You cannot directly access elements stored in the middle or at the bottom without first removing the elements above them.Real-World Analogy: A stack of dinner plates or a deck of cards—you place plates on top and take them off from the top.2. Core Operations & Time ComplexityPush (Insert): $\mathcal{O}(1)$ — Adds an element to the top of the stack.Pop (Delete): $\mathcal{O}(1)$ — Removes the top element from the stack.Peek / Top (Access): $\mathcal{O}(1)$ — Returns the value of the top element without removing it.IsEmpty / IsFull: $\mathcal{O}(1)$ — Checks whether the stack has zero elements or has reached its capacity limit.Search: $\mathcal{O}(N)$ — Requires popping elements sequentially to locate a target value.3. Real-World ApplicationsFunction Call Stack: Manages active functions, local variables, and return addresses during program execution (recursion tracking).Undo / Redo Mechanisms: Tracks user operations in text editors and graphic software so changes can be reverted in reverse order.Syntax Parsing & Balanced Parentheses: Verifies balanced symbols (e.g., matching {, [, () in compilers and interpreters.Expression Evaluation: Converts and evaluates expressions between Infix, Postfix, and Prefix notations.4. Complete C++ Implementation (Array-Based)C++#include <iostream>

class Stack {
private:
    int* data;
    int topIndex;
    int capacity;

public:
    Stack(int cap) : capacity(cap), topIndex(-1) {
        data = new int[capacity];
    }

    ~Stack() {
        delete[] data;
    }

    bool IsFull() const {
        return topIndex == capacity - 1;
    }

    bool IsEmpty() const {
        return topIndex == -1;
    }

    bool Push(int value) {
        if (IsFull()) {
            std::cout << "Stack Overflow! Cannot push " << value << "\n";
            return false;
        }
        data[++topIndex] = value;
        return true;
    }

    bool Pop() {
        if (IsEmpty()) {
            std::cout << "Stack Underflow! Stack is empty.\n";
            return false;
        }
        --topIndex;
        return true;
    }

    int Top() const {
        if (IsEmpty()) {
            std::cout << "Stack is empty.\n";
            return -1;
        }
        return data[topIndex];
    }

    void Print() const {
        if (IsEmpty()) {
            std::cout << "Stack: [Empty]\n";
            return;
        }
        std::cout << "Stack (Top to Bottom): ";
        for (int i = topIndex; i >= 0; --i) {
            std::cout << data[i] << " ";
        }
        std::cout << "\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Stack s(5);

    // Push operations
    s.Push(10);
    s.Push(20);
    s.Push(30);
    s.Print(); // Output: 30 20 10

    // Top element
    std::cout << "Current Top: " << s.Top() << "\n"; // Output: 30

    // Pop operation
    s.Pop();
    s.Print(); // Output: 20 10

    std::cout << "New Top after Pop: " << s.Top() << "\n"; // Output: 20

    return 0;
}
5. SummaryLIFO Rule: The newest item is always processed first.
High Efficiency: Core actions (Push, Pop, Top) execute in deterministic $\mathcal{O}(1)$ constant time with no memory shifting required.Underflow & Overflow: Stacks must handle boundary conditions when attempting to pop from an empty structure (underflow) or push into a full, fixed-size structure (overflow).
